// std/strings — the operations `==` does not give you.
//
// The language gives `string` equality by contents and stops there, because
// it says nothing about collation and inventing an ordering is not a
// compiler's business. C has one, so this package borrows it.
//
// **This package used to be a wrapper over `<string.h>` and is not any more.**
//
// Every parameter here was declared `cstring`, and a `string` crossed into one
// as its `ptr` half with no cast and no check — which was safe only because
// a `\0` at `ptr[len]` was guaranteed. That conversion was deleted and
// the wrappers went with it: `strcmp`, `strncmp`, `strstr` and `strchr` are
// now written in Dado, ten lines of loops over bytes this package can already
// index.
//
// That is a better package and not a consolation. `strcmp` answered the C
// library's question — *how do these compare up to the first `\0`* — and every
// function here was quietly wrong for a string holding an interior one, which
// `#len` of a string had already stopped being wrong about. It also means a program that
// compares strings no longer carries `<string.h>`, so it stays freestanding
// — the freestanding rule, now reaching one more package.
//
// Parsing is not this package's: since `STR-FREESTANDING` `to_i32` and
// `to_f64` are **`core:strings/parse`**'s. They moved with the
// `private foreign "stdlib.h"` block they were the only users of, which is
// why the move was the fix rather than a tidy-up; that package has since
// replaced `atoi` and `atof` with Dado and includes nothing.
//
// **Who ends what this package returns.** A window (`substring`, `rest`,
// `trim`, the splitters, `bytes`) allocates nothing and is good as long as the
// bytes it was cut from; new text (`concat`, `join`, a `Builder`'s `finish`)
// is a `ref []char8` from the ambient allocator, so under a `using` arena you
// never free it and outside one the caller `#delete`s it.
package strings

// **This package now emits no `#include` at all** (`STR-FREESTANDING`,
// 2026-09-02), which is a stronger claim than the two rows that led here made
// separately, and the mechanism is worth stating once because it is not
// obvious.
//
// A `foreign` block's `#include` is emitted for **every importer of the package
// that holds the block**, whether or not anything in the program reaches a
// declaration inside it: the checker collects the header of every `foreign`
// block in every live unit, and the C emitter writes one line per header it is
// handed. Nothing between them asks whether the block was used. So a `foreign` block is a **property of its package**,
// not of the call that reaches it, and the only way a library declines to force
// a header on a caller is to put the block in a package the caller can decline.
//
// That is not a defect the emitter can fix on its own, and this comment says so
// because the obvious reading is that it can. Suppressing the `#include` of a
// block nothing reached would need the compiler to know that nothing reached
// it, and it does not: liveness is **package**-granularity by construction — a
// package that names another anywhere in any of its files keeps it live,
// whether or not the naming declaration is itself reachable. Worse, the emitter
// writes **every** function of every live package (its only exemption is an
// uncalled template), so `to_i32`'s body — `return atoi(s)` — was in the
// emitted C of every importer regardless. The block was never *unused* in
// anything the emitted C could see, and dropping its `#include` would have
// produced a translation unit that does not compile. Declaration-granularity
// liveness **and** function-level dead-code elimination are what that fix
// costs; moving one block costs a new package and three call sites.
//
// **`<string.h>` is gone from this package** (C-CSTRING, 2026-09-01), and the
// header comment above was one release out of date the whole time it stood.
//
// It said `strlen` was why a `cstring` could be measured and `memcpy` was why
// `clone_from_cstring` could copy — *"TOY cannot read a `cstring`'s bytes
// itself"*. Dado can: `string(c)` is a borrowing conversion that keeps the
// pointer and counts the length once,
// with the walk emitted as `dado_rt__clen` — a byte loop the compiler writes,
// not `strlen`. So both borrowings were paid for by a claim that had already
// been retired, and the price was the whole package: **importing `core:strings`
// put `#include <string.h>` in the emitted C**, which is not in C99 §4p6's
// freestanding set, so no `#NONE` program could use this package's `cstring`
// half at all. That is what took 11 `memcpy` → 0 to remove
// from the *compiler's* emission, and this package was quietly putting it back
// for anyone who imported it.
//
// Both are byte loops now (`#len` of a borrow, `clone_from_cstring`), which is exactly
// what is already claimed of `format`: *"its counted copies and a
// `cstring`'s length are hand-written byte loops rather than `memcpy` and
// `strlen`"*. The claim is true of this package now too.

// Ordering: byte by byte, and the shorter one first where one is a prefix of
// the other. Normalised to -1, 0, 1 — which `strcmp` was not, since C promises
// only the *sign*, so a program comparing its result against a number was
// relying on something no standard says.
//
// **Bytes and not characters.** This orders by the numbers in the string, which
// is what a comparison of `[]char8` can mean; it is not a collation and does
// not claim to be. Inventing one is not a compiler's business and borrowing
// C's turned out to be borrowing a different question.
i32 compare(string8 a, string8 b):
    i64 n = #len(a)
    i64 m = #len(b)
    i64 k = n
    if m < k: k = m
    for i in 0..<k:
        if a[i] < b[i]: return 0 - 1
        if a[i] > b[i]: return 1
    if n < m: return 0 - 1
    if n > m: return 1
    return 0

bool before(string8 a, string8 b):
    return compare(a, b) < 0

bool after(string8 a, string8 b):
    return compare(a, b) > 0

bool starts_with(string8 s, string8 prefix):
    i64 m = #len(prefix)
    if m > #len(s): return false
    for i in 0..<m:
        if s[i] != prefix[i]: return false
    return true

// The naive search, which is the right one at this size: no table to build, no
// allocation, and the corpus's needles are words. `strstr` may do better on a
// long haystack and it also stopped at the first interior `\0`, which is the
// trade this package is no longer making.
bool contains(string8 haystack, string8 needle):
    i64 n = #len(haystack)
    i64 m = #len(needle)
    if m == 0: return true
    if m > n: return false
    for at in 0..<((n - m) + 1):
        bool same = true
        for i in 0..<m:
            if haystack[at + i] != needle[i]:
                same = false
        if same: return true
    return false

// Searching for `'\0'` finds an interior one and reports it, where `strchr`
// found the terminator. That is the same correction `#len` of a string made and it goes
// the same way: this package answers about the bytes the string has.
bool has_byte(string8 s, char8 byte):
    return index_of(s, byte) >= 0

bool ends_with(string8 s, string8 suffix):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    i64 m = #len(suffix)
    if m > n: return false
    for i in 0..<m:
        if s[(n - m) + i] != suffix[i]: return false
    return true

// **`#len(s) == 0` and not `s == ""`**, which is the same answer and a cheaper
// one — and, since C-CSTRING, the last `<string.h>` this package could put in
// an importer's emitted C.
//
// **It was not the last thing standing between this package and a `#NONE`
// target**, and the sentence that said so was falsified by the same commit
// that made it true of `<string.h>`. The `private foreign "stdlib.h"` block
// that used to sit at the top of this file — `atoi` and `atof` — was a
// `foreign` header, so it was emitted for anyone who imported `core:strings`
// whether or not they parsed anything. Measured on
// `--target=x86_64-unknown-none`, 2026-09-01: a program whose only use of this
// package was `strings.length` got `#include <stdint.h>`, `<stddef.h>` **and
// `<stdlib.h>`** where the same program without the import got `<stdint.h>`
// alone; `<stdlib.h>` is not in C99 §4p6's freestanding set either. That was a
// different shape of problem from this one and did not have the same fix: this
// line was a helper the *emitter* reached for on the package's behalf, and that
// one was a header the package itself wrote down. `STR-FREESTANDING` closed it
// by moving the block to `core:strings/parse` — measured again on the same
// triple and the same program, the import now costs `<stdint.h>` and
// `<stddef.h>`, both freestanding, and nothing else.
//
// The two are equivalent by construction: Dado's string equality is
// `a.len == b.len && memcmp(a.ptr, b.ptr, a.len) == 0` and `""` has length
// zero, so the `memcmp` is over zero bytes and can only agree. What it costs is
// the call — and the call is a `memcmp`, which is the **one** `<string.h>`
// spelling deliberately left standing in the emitter. It
// was left standing for a program that compares strings, which is a program
// that asked for it. This one line made every importer of `core:strings` pay
// it, whether or not it compared anything, because the emitter writes the whole
// package and the include follows the helper.
//
// It is also one hazard shorter: `memcmp(NULL, NULL, 0)` is undefined in C99
// even though every implementation returns 0, and a zero-length `string` is
// exactly what the `cstring` boundary below hands back.
bool is_empty(string8 s):
    return #len(s) == 0

// Where `byte` first occurs, or -1. Written by hand rather than through
// `strchr`, because C hands back a *pointer* and turning that into an index is
// pointer subtraction, which Dado does not have.
i64 index_of(string8 s, char8 byte):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    for i in 0..<n:
        if s[i] == byte: return i
    return 0 - 1

// Where it last occurs, or -1. Ranges are ascending only, so the
// walk counts down through a descending index rather than iterating backwards.
i64 last_index_of(string8 s, char8 byte):
    i64 i = #len(s)
    for i > 0:
        i = i - 1
        if s[i] == byte: return i
    return 0 - 1

// How many times `byte` occurs. The one a program splitting on a separator
// asks first, to know how many pieces it is about to walk.
i64 count_byte(string8 s, char8 byte):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    i64 seen = 0
    for i in 0..<n:
        if s[i] == byte: seen = seen + 1
    return seen

// ── the slicing half ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **This section is what the milestone was for, and it used to be a paragraph
// explaining why it could not exist.** That paragraph said `substring`, `trim`
// and `split` all return *new bytes* with nowhere to put them, and — the part
// that actually bound — that *"a 'cheap' substring that just narrows `ptr` and
// `len` would break the terminator invariant, which every call into C
// depends on, so the cheap version is not available either"*.
//
// Every clause of that was downstream of one guarantee. It is gone; a window
// over a `string` is a `string`, and the cheap version is now the
// only version. **Not one function in this section allocates**; each returns a window
// into the bytes it was given, and the caller's string is what owns them.
//
// The consequence to keep in mind is the one the old paragraph was protecting
// against: a window is not terminated, so none of these results may be handed
// to C. `clone_to_cstring` is the crossing, and it is where the copy is.

// Whether `b` is one of the four bytes this package treats as space.
//
// Written here rather than borrowed from `core:chars`, and **the reason given
// for that until 2026-09-02 was false**: it said *"no package under `core:`
// imports another"*, which is not a rule this library follows — `core:cfg`
// imports four, `core:log`, `core:net` and `core:http` each import this one,
// and `core:mem/arena` imports `core:mem`. The true reason is narrower and is
// about **this** package: `core:chars` is a wrapper over `<ctype.h>`, so
// borrowing `isspace` from it would put a hosted header in every importer of
// `core:strings` by exactly the mechanism `STR-FREESTANDING` and `C-CSTRING`
// spent two rows removing. Four byte comparisons is what that costs, and it is
// cheap. `core:strings` importing **nothing** is the property; it is not a
// rule about `core:` and never was.
bool is_space(char8 b):
    return b == ' ' || b == '\t' || b == '\n' || b == '\r'

// `count` bytes of `s` from `at`, **clamped** rather than refused.
//
// The clamp is not what keeps this safe. A window is bounds-checked where it
// is cut, computed bounds included: `s[at..<at + count]` past the end stops
// the program with *window out of bounds* and the Dado source location of the
// slice, and `--no-assert` does not remove the check.
// So taking these bounds literally would be safe too — it would trap.
//
// The clamp is the API's choice, and it is about the common call: a bounded
// read of text whose length the caller has not measured, *up to `count` bytes
// from here*. A `substring` that trapped on a count past the end would make
// every such call write the `min` by hand first. Clamped, the result is always
// a window into `s`, and a caller who asked for more than there is gets what
// there is. A caller who needs to know whether it got all of it compares the
// result's length with `count`.
string8 substring(string8 s, i64 at, i64 count):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    i64 start = at
    if start < 0: start = 0
    if start > n: start = n
    i64 stop = start + count
    if count < 0: stop = start
    if stop < start: stop = start
    if stop > n: stop = n
    return s[start..<stop]

// Everything of `s` from `at` to the end: `substring(s, at, #len(s))` said
// **to the end** by passing a count long enough to be clamped, and read as
// though the third argument were an end index — which is the misreading
// `substring`'s count invites. `at` is clamped to `0..n` the same way, so
// the result is always a window into `s`.
//
// `substring` keeps its clamp. A count past the end is a caller asking for
// more than there is, and refusing it would make every bounded read of
// untrusted text write the `min` by hand; what the clamp hid was only the
// spelling of *to the end*, and that now has its own name.
string8 rest(string8 s, i64 at):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    i64 start = at
    if start < 0: start = 0
    if start > n: start = n
    return s[start..<n]

string8 trim_left(string8 s):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    i64 at = 0
    for at < n && is_space(s[at]):
        at = at + 1
    return s[at..<n]

string8 trim_right(string8 s):
    i64 stop = #len(s)
    for stop > 0 && is_space(s[stop - 1]):
        stop = stop - 1
    return s[0..<stop]

string8 trim(string8 s):
    return trim_left(trim_right(s))

// The **cursor pair**, and the shape every splitter here takes.
//
// `head` is the bytes before the first `sep`, `rest` is everything after it,
// and `found` says whether there was one. When there was not, `head` is the
// whole string and `rest` is empty — so a caller that ignores `found` still
// terminates, which is the property that makes the loop below safe to write
// carelessly.
//
//     string rest = line
//     bool more = true
//     for more:
//         (string word, string tail, bool found) = split_first(rest, " ")
//         use(word)
//         rest = tail
//         more = found
//
// **Why a cursor and not a `[]string`.** A function returning a collection has
// to allocate one, which would make this the only thing in the package that
// does, and would need an allocator on a call that is otherwise free. Dado has
// no user-definable iterator either — `for x in y` walks the shapes the
// language knows and nothing else — so the third option was a language
// feature rather than a library one. A pair of windows is neither: it costs
// nothing, needs nothing new, and composes.
//
// An empty `sep` finds nothing, because every position matches one and
// answering the first would return an empty `head` forever.
(string8 head, string8 rest, bool found) split_first(string8 s, string8 sep):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    i64 m = #len(sep)
    if m == 0 || m > n:
        return (s, s[n..<n], false)
    for at in 0..<((n - m) + 1):
        bool same = true
        for i in 0..<m:
            if s[at + i] != sep[i]:
                same = false
        if same:
            return (s[0..<at], s[(at + m)..<n], true)
    return (s, s[n..<n], false)

// One line, and the rest. `\n` is the separator and a trailing `\r` is dropped,
// so a file written on either kind of machine reads the same.
(string8 head, string8 rest, bool found) line(string8 s):
    (string8 first, string8 tail, bool found) = split_first(s, "\n")
    return (trim_right_cr(first), tail, found)

// `trim_right`'s one-byte cousin, kept separate because `line` must not eat
// real trailing space — only the carriage return that belongs to the newline.
string8 trim_right_cr(string8 s):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    if n > 0 && s[n - 1] == '\r':
        return s[0..<(n - 1)]
    return s

// One whitespace-separated field, and the rest. Leading space is skipped, so
// runs of space between fields do not produce empty ones — which is the whole
// difference between this and `split_first(s, " ")`, and is what a caller
// splitting a line of input almost always wants.
(string8 head, string8 rest, bool found) field(string8 s):
    string8 from = trim_left(s)
    i64 n = #len(from)
    if n == 0:
        return (from, from, false)
    i64 at = 0
    for at < n && !is_space(from[at]):
        at = at + 1
    return (from[0..<at], from[at..<n], at < n)

// Where `window`'s bytes start inside `parent`'s, and whether they lie
// within them at all — so the splitters above **compose**. Every one of them
// returns a window and none says where in its input it came from, so
// *"from where this line started to where the next word ends"* had no
// spelling: a window's bounds are its own, and indexing past them is refused.
// With the offset, the span is `parent[a..<(b + #len(word))]`, cut from the
// parent, which is the one string whose bounds cover it.
//
// **An address comparison, not a search.** Two windows with the same bytes
// at different places are different answers, and a window equal to a
// substring of `parent` but cut from another buffer is `false`. The
// comparison is made on `#c.uintptr_t`s, the one crossing from an address to
// a number Dado has: ordering two C pointers into different objects is
// undefined, and `window` is not known to be inside `parent` until this has
// answered. An empty window at `parent`'s end answers `#len(parent)`, which
// is where `rest(parent, #len(parent))` would have cut it.
(i64 at, bool ok) offset_in(string8 parent, string8 window):
    #c.uintptr_t p = address(parent)
    #c.uintptr_t w = address(window)
    if w < p:
        return (0, false)
    #c.uintptr_t gap = w - p
    #c.uintptr_t limit = #c.uintptr_t(#len(parent))
    if gap > limit:
        return (0, false)
    i64 at = i64(gap)
    if #len(window) > #len(parent) - at:
        return (0, false)
    return (at, true)

// The address of `s`'s first byte, as a number. `&b[0]` is legal on an empty
// window — an address-of subscript may name the end — so this answers for
// `""` too.
private #c.uintptr_t address(string8 s):
    []char8 b = bytes(s)
    return #c.uintptr_t(rawptr(&b[0]))

// ── building new text ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **The ownership convention, in one sentence: a function here that makes new
// bytes returns them as a `ref []char8` allocated from the ambient allocator
// (`#default`, which `using` sets), so under a `using` arena you never free
// it and outside one you `#delete` it; `string8(r[:])` reads it as text.**
//
// This section used to be a paragraph declining `join` and the rest, because
// *"those genuinely do return new bytes, so each needs an allocator and a
// `delete`"*. Both halves of that are answered without a parameter: the
// allocator is the ambient one — which is what `using` is for — and the
// `delete` is possible because what comes back is a **`ref`**, the one thing
// with authority to free. A `string8` would not have been: it is a window,
// `#delete` of a window is ERR0504, and a function that allocated one and
// handed out only the window would make bytes nobody can end. That is
// exactly what `core:cfg`'s values once were, and it is why `join` answers
// a run and not a string.
//
// The C-boundary pair below (`clone_to_cstring`, `clone_from_cstring`)
// predates the convention and takes its allocator as a parameter; it is left
// as it is, and a `using` reaches it only through a caller passing
// `#default`.

// `Bytes` is the name the `string8 → []char8` conversion is reached through:
// the conversion is free, but `[]char8(s)` is not a callable spelling.
type Bytes: []char8

// `s`'s bytes, as a window over them. **A pure reinterpret**: the two words
// are the same two words and nothing is copied or allocated. What is given up
// is only the claim that the bytes are text, which is why the result is a
// `[]char8`: indexable, and not something a `cstring` parameter accepts. The
// bytes are still `s`'s, so a write through the result writes whatever `s` is
// a window into — a literal's bytes are read-only.
[]char8 bytes(string8 s):
    return Bytes(s)

// A new run holding `a` then `b`. See the convention above: allocated from
// `#default`, freed by `#delete` or by the end of the `using` that placed it.
// `nil` when the allocation fails.
ref []char8 concat(string8 a, string8 b):
    i64 n = #len(a)
    i64 m = #len(b)
    ref []char8 out = #make([]char8, n + m)
    if out == nil:
        return out
    for i in 0..<n:
        out[i] = a[i]
    for i in 0..<m:
        out[n + i] = b[i]
    return out

// Every part, with `sep` between each two. `join([], sep)` is an empty run,
// not `nil`; `nil` means the allocation failed. One allocation: the length is
// counted first.
ref []char8 join([]string8 parts, string8 sep):
    i64 total = 0
    for p, i in parts:
        if i > 0:
            total = total + #len(sep)
        total = total + #len(p)
    ref []char8 out = #make([]char8, total)
    if out == nil:
        return out
    i64 at = 0
    for p, i in parts:
        if i > 0:
            for k in 0..<#len(sep):
                out[at + k] = sep[k]
            at = at + #len(sep)
        for k in 0..<#len(p):
            out[at + k] = p[k]
        at = at + #len(p)
    return out

// Text built a piece at a time, when the pieces are not all known at once.
//
// **A `Builder` is its run and nothing else**, so it is freed the way any run
// is: `#delete(b)`, or the end of the `using` it was made under. `finish`
// hands the run over and leaves the builder `nil`, so a builder that was
// finished and a builder that was never made are both safe to `#delete`.
// `text` reads what is there so far, as a window that the next `append` may
// move.
//
//     strings.Builder b = strings.builder()
//     _ = strings.append(&b, "key")
//     _ = strings.append_char(&b, '=')
//     _ = strings.append(&b, value)
//     ref []char8 line = strings.finish(&b)
//     defer #delete(line)
type Builder: ref []char8

// An empty builder with room for `room` bytes before its first growth,
// allocated from `#default`. `nil` when the allocation fails, and every
// `append` to a `nil` builder answers `false`.
Builder builder(i64 room = 16):
    ref []char8 run = #make([]char8, 0)
    if run == nil:
        return run
    _ = #reserve(run, room)
    return run

// `s` on the end. `false`, and nothing appended, when the run could not grow.
bool append(^Builder b, string8 s):
    if b^ == nil:
        return false
    i64 n = #len(s)
    if !#reserve(b^, #len(b^) + n):
        return false
    for i in 0..<n:
        _ = #append(b^, s[i])
    return true

// One byte on the end. `false`, and nothing appended, when the run could not
// grow.
bool append_char(^Builder b, char8 c):
    if b^ == nil:
        return false
    return #append(b^, c)

// What has been built so far, as text. A window into the builder's run, so it
// is good until the next `append`, `finish` or `#delete`.
string8 text(Builder b):
    return string8(b[:])

// The run, handed over: the caller owns it now (see the convention above),
// and `b` is left `nil`.
//
// Written with a `defer` rather than as `out = b^; b^ = nil; return out`,
// which the provenance pass (ERR0803) refuses: it reads a `ref` copied out of
// `b^` as a view minted from it, so the rebinding looks like a move under it.
ref []char8 finish(^Builder b):
    defer b^ = nil
    return b^

// ── parsing lives next door ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `to_i32` and `to_f64` are **`core:strings/parse`** since `STR-FREESTANDING`
// (2026-09-02), when they were C's `atoi` and `atof` behind a `foreign` block
// and moving them out was what kept `<stdlib.h>` off this package's importers.
// Both are Dado now and that package includes nothing either; it stayed a
// package of its own because a parser is not a string operation.
//
//     import "core:strings"
//     import "core:strings/parse"
//     ...
//     i32 n = parse.to_i32(cstring(c))
//     try f64 x = parse.to_f64(s) else code:
//         ...
//
// `to_i32` still takes a `cstring` and still answers `atoi`'s forgiving 0;
// `to_f64` takes a `string8` and fails, through `!`, on anything that is not
// exactly a number.

// ── reading a `cstring` ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **A `cstring` gets *both* the
// measure-and-copy and the no-copy view.** The copy is the safe default and
// what a caller should reach for first; the view is allowed on the ground that
// the compiler guarantees only what falls out of the type system and the rest
// is tooling's — because otherwise every caller who only wants to **compare**
// a `char *` pays an allocation.
//
// The view is a **borrow**, with everything a borrow cannot do: it is not
// `delete`d, not `resize`d, and not returned out of the block that owns the
// bytes. The first two the compiler already refuses — `delete` of a window is
// `ERR0504` (*"a `^T` and a `[]T` are **views**: TOY did not allocate what they
// name and cannot free it"*), and so is `resize`. The third the compiler
// **cannot** check here and does not claim to: the bytes belong to C, so how
// long they last is C's answer and not the type's. That is the division, and it
// is the whole of what this package promises. Nothing more is implied.
//
// **The three below are library functions and not builtins**, and the argument
// is that the global namespace is freestanding-total, and the
// name a program reaches for with no import must work on a target with no
// heap. `clone_from_cstring` **allocates**, so it cannot be in that namespace;
// putting the copy in the library and the borrow in the global namespace would
// split one boundary across two lookup rules for no reason a reader could
// state. So all of it is here. The **conversion** `string(c)` stays a
// conversion — it is a retag and a walk, it allocates nothing, and it is the
// language's spelling of the borrow; `borrow_from_cstring` is what it should
// mean when `c` may be `nil`, which is the routine case at this boundary.
//
// **`nil` is answered once, and identically for both halves: a zero-length
// `string`, never a fault and never a refusal.** The argument, in three parts:
//
//  1. *A refusal at the type level cannot be written.* `nil` is a run-time
//     value of `cstring` — `getenv` returns it, `argv[argc]` is it, `fopen`'s
//     failure is it — and the type does not carry which. A refusal would have
//     to be a refusal of `string(c)` outright, which would take the borrow
//     away from the callers the ruling is for.
//  2. *The tree already answers zero, twice.* measuring `nil` was 0 before this
//     row and `clone_from_cstring(nil, …)` was an empty run; `println(c)` on a
//     `nil` emits `fputs(c ? c : "", stdout)`, and the emitter's own comment on
//     that guard says *"the `? :` is not defensiveness: `NULL` is what a C
//     function returns when it has nothing to say"*. Answering anything else
//     here would make this package the one place that disagreed.
//  3. *The alternative that was rejected.* A `nil` `^u8` windowed by
//     `p[0..<16]` emits `.ptr = NULL, .len = 16` today and runs — a length the
//     compiler asserts and no memory backs. That is the shape not to
//     reproduce, and a zero length over `""` is its opposite: the count is
//     true of the bytes the result points at.
//
// **Not the same answer as `!= nil`.** A zero-length borrow does not say
// whether `c` was `nil` or was `""`, and neither does its `#len`. A caller who
// needs that distinction asks `c == nil` directly, which is the question, and
// `corpus/hosted` is where it is asked.

// The bytes at `c`, as a `string`, **without copying them**.
//
// One walk to find the length C threw away, and then the same pointer: the
// result is two words over C's buffer and this function allocates nothing.
//
// **This is `string(c)` with the `nil` written out**, and since C-CSTRING
// that is *all* it is. This comment used to say the conversion did not guard,
// that `string(c)` on a `nil` walked from address zero and faulted, and that
// the defect was filed and would one day be fixed. **It was fixed in the same
// change this function landed with.** The borrow now emits
// `{ .ptr = c ? c : "", .len = c ? dado_clen(c) : 0 }` — one temporary, three
// reads of it, still exactly one walk — so a `nil` borrows as the empty
// string, which is the answer the `format` path in the same emitter always
// gave. Measured: `string(getenv("NOT_SET"))` has length 0 and runs.
//
// So the body below is a restatement of the conversion rather than a repair
// of it. It stays as it is, and the name stays: what it buys a reader is that the `nil` is *written down* at the
// boundary where it is the routine case, instead of being a property of the
// emitter they would have to know.
//
// **The result must not outlive C's buffer**, and nothing here can check that.
// `getenv`'s and `argv`'s bytes belong to the process and outlive any Dado
// frame; a buffer a C call filled and will reuse does not. When in doubt the
// answer is `clone_from_cstring` below, which is why that one is the default.
string8 borrow_from_cstring(cstring c):
    if c == nil: return ""
    return string8(c)

// Copy `s`'s bytes into an owned run and put a `\0` after them, so the result
// can cross to C.
//
// **The allocation is the caller's**, which is the whole shape of this
// function and the reason it is not a `to_cstring(string)`. The objection to
// `format` is that it *"would have to choose one silently"*; a conversion that
// allocated out of some ambient pool would be the same objection with a
// different verb. The caller passes the allocator, so the caller can `delete`
// it, `resize` it, or hand it a bump arena that never frees.
//
// **`ref []char8` and not a boxed `cstring`.** An owning run is an ordinary
// reference: `delete`, `copy` and `resize` already work on it, `len` reads its
// length, and it can be returned, stored in a map, or held past the block that
// made it. Inventing a `cstring` that owns its bytes would have needed all four
// of those written again for one type.
//
// `len + 1` and not `len`: the terminator is a byte the C side reads and the
// Dado side never does. `#len(out)` therefore answers **one more** than
// `#len(s)`, which is the honest number — the run really is that long — and
// is the one thing about this function a caller has to know.
//
// Interior `\0`s are copied through rather than stopped at, because this
// function's job is the bytes it was given. A string holding one produces a run
// that C will read only up to that byte; that is C's rule, not this function's,
// and `#len` of a borrow is the measurement on the other side of it.
ref []char8 clone_to_cstring(string8 s, (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) alloc):
    i64 n = #len(s)
    ref []char8 out = #make([]char8, n + 1, alloc)
    for i in 0..<n:
        out[i] = s[i]
    out[n] = '\0'
    return out

// Copy a NUL-terminated C string into an owned run, so C's bytes become a value
// Dado owns. The inverse of `clone_to_cstring`.
//
// **The allocation is the caller's**, the same shape as `clone_to_cstring`: the
// caller passes the allocator and so can `delete`, `resize`, or hand it an
// arena. The result is an owning `ref []char8` — `delete` it when done, and
// read it as text with `string(out[:])`, which is free.
//
// **A copy, not a borrow, and that is the whole reason this is a function** —
// it is the half of the boundary that outlives C's buffer, and the default.
// `borrow_from_cstring` above is the other half and costs nothing; this one is
// what a caller reaches for when the bytes have to last longer than the call
// that produced them, or have to be written to.
//
// **The comment that stood here was two releases stale and is deleted.** It
// said *"TOY refuses to launder one into a `rawptr` or a `string`, and the
// borrowing `string(c)` should be allowed and is not yet"*. It **is** allowed
// now: `string(c)` is a borrowing conversion
// and has been since before this milestone. Nothing about this function's
// shape depended on the refusal, but its `strlen` and its `memcpy` did — both
// were there because the package believed it could not read C's bytes itself.
//
// **The copy is a byte loop**, the same loop `clone_to_cstring` takes in the
// other direction, over the borrow. The length is one walk (`len` of the
// borrow) and the bytes are moved one at a time, so nothing here needs
// `<string.h>` and a `#NONE` program may call it. The run outlives the C buffer
// and does not alias it, which is what "owned" buys.
//
// **`nil` yields an empty run**, matching a `nil` borrow's `#len` of 0 and
// `borrow_from_cstring(nil) == ""`: one answer for the whole boundary, argued
// at the section head above. The terminator is **not** copied — this returns
// the bytes, and `#len(out)` is `#len` of the borrow, one less than
// `clone_to_cstring`'s output for the same text.
ref []char8 clone_from_cstring(cstring c, (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) alloc):
    string8 s = borrow_from_cstring(c)
    i64 n = #len(s)
    ref []char8 out = #make([]char8, n, alloc)
    for i in 0..<n:
        out[i] = s[i]
    return out
